what the crown has always done so  cleverly I think is not tried to imitate  the characters but to sort of evoke them  the crown season five is here and just  like you expected this season is full of  attention to detail and underlying  themes so stick around for all of the  details that went into creating the  crown season five number one the slowest  makeover Ever that's one way you might  describe what Diana went through in  season 5 of the crown to help Mark the  emotional journey of Diana's character  costume designers came up with some  rules for how to dress her in the first  few episodes she wears long skirts and  baggy sweaters softer shapes that  suggest a formlessness or a sense of  hiding from the world in later episodes  her skirts get shorter and her  silhouette becomes simpler and more  defined to show a stripped back Diana  regaining control of her life it's kind  of an evolution story in the sense of  watching somebody sort of find their  Center again Elizabeth Debicki wasn't  the only one undergoing unusual  makeovers this season Johnny Lee Miller  had more than a few special makeup  treatments thanks to some frequent black  eyes and bruising but more on that later  number two turn the music up Johnny Lee  Miller played John Major but he also  played the Royal DJ on set for season  five of the crown Johnny would help pass  the time during things like lengthy wig  fitting sessions by DJing for the crew  he also kept the makeup artists busy as  an avid martial artist Johnny would  often come to set with a black eye he  once even had his finger tattooed  between shooting days isn't there a rule  about that somewhere I'm quite relaxed  but yeah it's just bizarre Johnny seems  to be a far cry from the quiet and  gentle stealiness of John Major number  three hold your horses or let them ride  the crown season 5 actor Jonathan price  was taught to handle horses by some of  Prince Philip's real life racing friends  for the carriage racing scenes in this  season although Jonathan learned to  handle reigns with real horses in the  scene from the show The Carriage is  actually pulled by a cam camera truck  better safe than sorry number four  things got a little too real this season  the crown has made use of an elaborate  collection of props and set pieces some  were created for the show and some were  actually antiques production for the  crown had a major setback when 150  000 pounds worth of props were stolen  from production trucks set decorator  Allison Harvey recalled one of the worst  things that happened this season was  three of the prop trucks with our  antiques got broken into overnight all  the Antiques were stolen Harvey  explained it was obviously a nightmare  because we had to try to replace them  quickly when the crime was reported the  police said they didn't have time to  investigate the theft because they had  found a body in the river nearby the  body turned out to be a prosthetic  belonging to the production team of the  crown and was one of the items stolen  from the back of the trucks we'll try to  get it back actually really the police  still got it number five is that a smirk  or a quirk all of the actors on season 5  had to undergo some fairly intense  transfer formations to play these iconic  characters convincingly and while  sometimes these Transformations can be  complicated other times there are simple  short tricks I suppose it starts with a  jaw and it's so sort of because the  jaw's so tight Dominic West says that  one of the secrets to speaking like  Charles was to talk slightly from the  side of his mouth Dominic describes the  motion as a man stopping words from  slipping out out of fear for saying the  wrong thing keeping it simple number six  you thought you spotted a few Grays  didn't you to help Mark the passage of  time over the years that the crown  covers the hair and makeup Department  would do things like use half a dozen  wigs on a single actor Imelda Staunton  wears six different wigs throughout the  series she starts with the same wig for  the six episodes gains a few white  pieces for the next three episodes and  has a wider lighter wig for episode 10  showing the passing of five years and  the toll that time has taken on the  queen number seven another season  another decade as the show moves into  the 90s the direct actors tried to mimic  the bright and colorful trends of the  era camera movements are more free and  the color palette in the opening  episodes is much lighter than previous  Seasons if you watch or re-watch  carefully you'll notice that this  colorful palette does fade over the  season to match the darker tone of the  drama number eight everything the light  touches and some of those things in the  dark too set decorator Alison Harvey was  in charge of creating a believable world  for the crown actors to live in and that  proved to be a challenge all of its own  hopefully by the end of it it looks  like someone lives there Allison  describes the approach to creating a  world that is both believable for the  viewers and inhabitable for the actors  we start with the flooring the lighting  the big lumps of furniture ultimately  you end up with a paper knife it's like  moving house every day Allison's  expertise was used not only to create a  believable world for viewers like you to  enjoy but for the actors to believe they  were really a part of it we can only  imagine the secrets buried in each set  and prop piece and we can probably all  agree with Dominic West when he says so  much goes unseen and unthanked number  nine have you got those Royal moves you  might say the cast of season five  certainly do thanks to their Royal  choreographer the cast worked with  movement coach Paulie Bennett to better  capture the performance of their  characters with body language Paulie  explains things like how Diana greets a  crowd from the back to the front  sometimes crouching to greet kids up  close for Elizabeth whenever she had to  do those big crowd scenes it wasn't an  overwhelming  experience Paulie also suggested to  Imelda Stanton that she should think of  pressing her big toes into the floor and  square off her hips in her stance in  order to portray the Queen's quiet  confidence in entering a room so just  those little things are sort of helpful  few for an actor on a film set number  10. Britannia rule the waves if you  watch carefully you might notice that  season 5 uses the Royal yacht called the  Britannia as a metaphor for the royal  family in the early 90s in the earlier  Seasons the Britannia was the Queen's  first home a place she had total control  over when she first sat upon the throne  in the later Seasons the yacht is in  disrepair just as the Royal Family's own  standing becomes tarnished in the show  just as people question the need for the  yacht the show depicts TV shows of the  time questioning the role of the royal  family number 11. the Apple doesn't fall  far from the tree in season 5 Prince  William is played by San and West who is  Dominic West's real-life son they said  we can't find a blue-eyed blonde boy 13  who's starting boarding school I said I  think I've got just the guy you might  say the two didn't have to do much  acting for their relationship as father  and son what's even cooler is that  playing Prince William on the crown is  sennon's first acting role proud father  Dominic said it was amazing suddenly  seeing his son on camera number 12.  James is that you you might have thought  there was something about Mr Townsend in  season five that's because former 007  Timothy Dalton joined the cast as Peter  Townsend to tell the story of his  reunion with Margaret after years apart  Timothy was suggested to the casting  team by Leslie Manville who plays  Margaret Leslie enjoyed the bond  connection saying it's very Margaret  isn't it to be dancing with James Bond  she'd have loved it number 13 wait for  real Dominic West had a secret advantage  while learning to play Charles he has  actually met the real-life counterpart  of his character several times West said  I've been in line to shake his hand a  few times and it's fascinating it's very  useful to meet the character you're  playing obviously another season of the  crown and yet another compelling  depiction of these historic characters  and with that another set of curious  stories around how the details of season  five were brought to life this is only a  short list of the tales from behind the  scenes so what did we miss what do you  think let us know in the comments below  [Music]

